# First MWGym experiment should actually be about this exact problem

Before Yu-Gi-Oh learning, I'd make an extremely boring calibration experiment.

Same 100 simple filesystem tasks.

Run four architectures:

| Arm | Runtime                     |
| --- | --------------------------- |
| A   | direct model, one shot      |
| B   | Letta stateless             |
| C   | Letta stateful              |
| D   | dynamic router choosing A/C |

Measure:

```text
success
model calls
tokens
latency
cost
```

We should expect A to dominate trivial tasks.

If D doesn't learn to choose A there, the economic allocator is already broken.

Then give increasingly complicated tasks where persistent context matters.

Eventually C should start winning.

The crossover point is interesting:

$$
V(memory) > Cost(memory)
$$

That's basically the first MWGym result.

